The \textbf{derivative} of a function $f$ at a point $x = a$ is defined as:
\[
\boxed{f'(a) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(a + h) - f(a)}{h}}
\]

provided this limit exists.

\textbf{Alternative form:}
\[
\boxed{f'(a) = \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Terminology:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If the limit exists, we say $f$ is \textbf{differentiable} at $x = a$
    \item $f'(a)$ is read as "$f$ prime of $a$"
    \item The process of finding $f'(a)$ is called \textbf{differentiation}
    \item Other notations: $\dfrac{df}{dx}\bigg|_{x=a}$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}\bigg|_{x=a}$, $Df(a)$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{The Difference Quotient:}}

The expression $\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}$ is called the \textbf{difference quotient}. It represents:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Numerator: Change in function value (rise)
    \item Denominator: Change in input value (run)
    \item Quotient: Average rate of change
\end{itemize}

Taking the limit as $h \to 0$ gives the \textbf{instantaneous rate of change}.

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is differentiable at every point in its domain, we can define the \textbf{derivative function} $f'(x)$:
\[
\boxed{f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}}
\]

\textbf{Common Notations:}
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{df}{dx} = \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{d}{dx}[f(x)] = Df(x) = D_x f(x)
\end{align*}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Understanding the Notation:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$: "dee y dee x" - Leibniz notation (most common)
    \item $f'(x)$: "f prime of x" - Lagrange notation
    \item $Df$: "D of f" - Operator notation
    \item The "$d$" represents an infinitesimally small change
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use the definition of the derivative to find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 3x + 2$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Write the definition
\[
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f(x + h)$
\begin{align*}
f(x + h) &= 3(x + h) + 2 \\
&= 3x + 3h + 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Compute the difference quotient
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} &= \frac{(3x + 3h + 2) - (3x + 2)}{h} \\
&= \frac{3x + 3h + 2 - 3x - 2}{h} \\
&= \frac{3h}{h} \\
&= 3 \quad \text{(for } h \neq 0\text{)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Take the limit
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} 3 \\
&= 3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 3}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Interpretation:}} The derivative is constant and equals 3, which is the slope of the line $y = 3x + 2$. This makes sense: linear functions have constant rate of change!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use the definition to find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x^2$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Write the definition
\[
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f(x + h)$
\begin{align*}
f(x + h) &= (x + h)^2 \\
&= x^2 + 2xh + h^2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Compute the difference quotient
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} &= \frac{(x^2 + 2xh + h^2) - x^2}{h} \\
&= \frac{x^2 + 2xh + h^2 - x^2}{h} \\
&= \frac{2xh + h^2}{h} \\
&= \frac{h(2x + h)}{h} \\
&= 2x + h \quad \text{(for } h \neq 0\text{)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Take the limit
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} (2x + h) \\
&= 2x + 0 \\
&= 2x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 2x}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} At $x = 3$: $f'(3) = 2(3) = 6$. This means at the point $(3, 9)$ on $y = x^2$, the instantaneous rate of change is 6.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Pattern:}} Notice that the derivative of $x^2$ is $2x$. This is a special case of the power rule!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x}$ using first principles.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Write the definition
\[
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f(x + h)$
\[
f(x + h) = \frac{1}{x + h}
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Compute the difference quotient
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} &= \frac{\frac{1}{x+h} - \frac{1}{x}}{h}
\end{align*}

Simplify the complex fraction (find common denominator in numerator):
\begin{align*}
&= \frac{\frac{x - (x+h)}{x(x+h)}}{h} \\
&= \frac{\frac{x - x - h}{x(x+h)}}{h} \\
&= \frac{\frac{-h}{x(x+h)}}{h} \\
&= \frac{-h}{x(x+h)} \cdot \frac{1}{h} \\
&= \frac{-h}{hx(x+h)} \\
&= \frac{-1}{x(x+h)} \quad \text{(for } h \neq 0\text{)}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Take the limit
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-1}{x(x+h)} \\
&= \frac{-1}{x(x+0)} \\
&= \frac{-1}{x^2} \\
&= -\frac{1}{x^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = -\frac{1}{x^2}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note:}} The derivative is always negative, which makes sense because $f(x) = \frac{1}{x}$ is decreasing on $(0, \infty)$ and on $(-\infty, 0)$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = \sqrt{x}$ using the definition.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Write the definition
\[
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Set up the difference quotient
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{\sqrt{x+h} - \sqrt{x}}{h}
\end{align*}

This gives $\frac{0}{0}$ form. Use rationalization!

\textbf{Step 3:} Rationalize the numerator

Multiply by conjugate:
\begin{align*}
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{\sqrt{x+h} - \sqrt{x}}{h} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x}} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{(\sqrt{x+h})^2 - (\sqrt{x})^2}{h(\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x})} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{(x+h) - x}{h(\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x})} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{h}{h(\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x})} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x+h} + \sqrt{x}}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Evaluate the limit
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{x+0} + \sqrt{x}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{x}} \\
&= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} This can also be written as $f'(x) = \frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}$, which matches the power rule: $\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) = \frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Domain Note:}} $f'(x)$ is defined only for $x > 0$ (same as domain of $\sqrt{x}$).

\vspace{0.5cm}

A function $f$ is \textbf{differentiable at} $x = a$ if $f'(a)$ exists (i.e., the limit defining the derivative exists and is finite).

A function is \textbf{differentiable on an interval} if it is differentiable at every point in that interval.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Important Relationship:}}

\textbf{Theorem:} If $f$ is differentiable at $x = a$, then $f$ is continuous at $x = a$.

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{Warning:}} The converse is NOT true! A function can be continuous but not differentiable.

\textbf{Example:} $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$ but not differentiable there (sharp corner).

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{When is a function NOT differentiable?}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item At a corner or cusp (like $|x|$ at $x = 0$)
    \item At a discontinuity
    \item At a vertical tangent line
    \item Where the function is not defined
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 2.1 Summary}}

\textbf{The Derivative Definition:}
\[
\boxed{f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Steps for Computing Derivatives from First Principles:}}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find $f(x + h)$ by substituting $x + h$ into the function
    \item Form the difference quotient: $\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$
    \item Simplify algebraically (factor, rationalize, combine fractions)
    \item Take the limit as $h \to 0$
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Key Derivatives from First Principles:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Function } $f(x)$ & \textbf{Derivative } $f'(x)$ \\
\hline
$c$ (constant) & $0$ \\
\hline
$x$ & $1$ \\
\hline
$x^2$ & $2x$ \\
\hline
$x^n$ & $nx^{n-1}$ \\
\hline
$\dfrac{1}{x}$ & $-\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ \\
\hline
$\sqrt{x}$ & $\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important Facts:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Differentiable $\implies$ Continuous
    \item Continuous $\not\!\!\!\implies$ Differentiable
    \item Not differentiable at: corners, cusps, discontinuities, vertical tangents
\end{itemize}

\newpage

The derivative $f'(a)$ represents the \textbf{slope of the tangent line} to the graph of $y = f(x)$ at the point $(a, f(a))$.

\textbf{Tangent Line Equation:}

The equation of the tangent line to $y = f(x)$ at $x = a$ is:
\[
\boxed{y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)}
\]

or in slope-intercept form:
\[
\boxed{y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Understanding the Tangent Line:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The tangent line is the best linear approximation to the curve at that point
    \item It "just touches" the curve at $(a, f(a))$
    \item The slope $m = f'(a)$ tells us the direction and steepness
    \item If $f'(a) > 0$: function is increasing at $x = a$ (upward slope)
    \item If $f'(a) < 0$: function is decreasing at $x = a$ (downward slope)
    \item If $f'(a) = 0$: horizontal tangent (possible max/min/inflection)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the equation of the tangent line to $y = x^2$ at the point $(2, 4)$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Find the derivative

From Example 2, we know $f'(x) = 2x$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Find the slope at $x = 2$
\[
f'(2) = 2(2) = 4
\]

So the slope of the tangent line is $m = 4$.

\textbf{Step 3:} Use point-slope form

The tangent line passes through $(2, 4)$ with slope $4$:
\begin{align*}
y - 4 &= 4(x - 2) \\
y - 4 &= 4x - 8 \\
y &= 4x - 4
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{y = 4x - 4}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Does it pass through $(2, 4)$? $y = 4(2) - 4 = 4$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item Does it have slope 4? Yes \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Visualization:}} At $x = 2$, the parabola $y = x^2$ is rising with slope 4. The tangent line $y = 4x - 4$ has the same slope and touches the parabola at exactly $(2, 4)$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

The \textbf{normal line} to a curve at a point is the line perpendicular to the tangent line at that point.

\textbf{Normal Line Equation:}

If the tangent line has slope $m = f'(a)$, the normal line has slope $m_{\text{normal}} = -\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}$ (negative reciprocal).

The equation of the normal line at $(a, f(a))$ is:
\[
\boxed{y - f(a) = -\frac{1}{f'(a)}(x - a)}
\]

provided $f'(a) \neq 0$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Perpendicular Slopes:}}

Two lines are perpendicular if and only if the product of their slopes is $-1$:
\[
m_1 \cdot m_2 = -1 \iff m_2 = -\frac{1}{m_1}
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Special Cases:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $f'(a) = 0$ (horizontal tangent), the normal line is vertical: $x = a$
    \item If tangent line is vertical, the normal line is horizontal
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

In physics and applied sciences, the derivative represents the \textbf{instantaneous rate of change}.

\textbf{Common Physical Interpretations:}

\textbf{1. Velocity (Physics):}

If $s(t)$ is the position of an object at time $t$, then:
\[
\boxed{v(t) = s'(t) = \frac{ds}{dt}}
\]
is the \textbf{velocity} (instantaneous rate of change of position).

\textbf{2. Acceleration (Physics):}

If $v(t)$ is the velocity at time $t$, then:
\[
\boxed{a(t) = v'(t) = \frac{dv}{dt} = s''(t) = \frac{d^2s}{dt^2}}
\]
is the \textbf{acceleration} (instantaneous rate of change of velocity).

\textbf{3. General Rate of Change:}

For any quantity $Q(t)$ depending on time:
\[
\boxed{\frac{dQ}{dt} = \text{instantaneous rate of change of } Q}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Sign Interpretation:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $v > 0$: moving forward (in positive direction)
    \item $v < 0$: moving backward (in negative direction)
    \item $v = 0$: instantaneously at rest
    \item $a > 0$: speeding up (if $v > 0$) or slowing down (if $v < 0$)
    \item $a < 0$: slowing down (if $v > 0$) or speeding up (if $v < 0$)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} The position of a particle moving along a line is given by $s(t) = t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t$ meters, where $t$ is in seconds.

(a) Find the velocity at time $t$.

(b) When is the particle at rest?

(c) Find the velocity at $t = 2$ seconds.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} Find velocity function

Velocity is the derivative of position:
\begin{align*}
v(t) &= s'(t) \\
&= \frac{d}{dt}(t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t) \\
&= 3t^2 - 12t + 9
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{v(t) = 3t^2 - 12t + 9 \text{ m/s}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} When is the particle at rest?

The particle is at rest when $v(t) = 0$:
\begin{align*}
3t^2 - 12t + 9 &= 0 \\
3(t^2 - 4t + 3) &= 0 \\
3(t - 1)(t - 3) &= 0
\end{align*}

Solutions: $t = 1$ or $t = 3$

\textbf{Answer:} The particle is at rest at \boxed{t = 1 \text{ s and } t = 3 \text{ s}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} Velocity at $t = 2$ seconds
\begin{align*}
v(2) &= 3(2)^2 - 12(2) + 9 \\
&= 12 - 24 + 9 \\
&= -3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{v(2) = -3 \text{ m/s}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Interpretation:}} At $t = 2$ seconds, the particle is moving backward (negative velocity) at 3 m/s.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Average Rate of Change:}

The average rate of change of $f(x)$ over the interval $[a, b]$ is:
\[
\boxed{\text{Average Rate} = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}}
\]

This is the slope of the \textbf{secant line} connecting $(a, f(a))$ and $(b, f(b))$.

\textbf{Instantaneous Rate of Change:}

The instantaneous rate of change at $x = a$ is:
\[
\boxed{\text{Instantaneous Rate} = f'(a) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}}
\]

This is the slope of the \textbf{tangent line} at $x = a$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Relationship:}}

The instantaneous rate of change is the limit of average rates of change as the interval shrinks to a point:
\[
f'(a) = \lim_{b \to a} \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} For $f(x) = x^2$:

(a) Find the average rate of change from $x = 1$ to $x = 3$.

(b) Find the instantaneous rate of change at $x = 1$.

(c) Find the instantaneous rate of change at $x = 3$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} Average rate of change from $x = 1$ to $x = 3$
\begin{align*}
\text{Average Rate} &= \frac{f(3) - f(1)}{3 - 1} \\
&= \frac{9 - 1}{2} \\
&= \frac{8}{2} \\
&= 4
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{4}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} Instantaneous rate at $x = 1$

We know $f'(x) = 2x$, so:
\[
f'(1) = 2(1) = 2
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{2}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} Instantaneous rate at $x = 3$
\[
f'(3) = 2(3) = 6
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{6}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Observation:}} The average rate of change (4) lies between the instantaneous rates at the endpoints (2 and 6). This is related to the Mean Value Theorem!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 2.2 Summary}}

\textbf{Geometric Interpretations:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|p{9cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Concept} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
$f'(a)$ & Slope of tangent line at $(a, f(a))$ \\
\hline
Tangent Line & $y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)$ \\
\hline
Normal Line & $y - f(a) = -\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}(x - a)$ (perpendicular to tangent) \\
\hline
$f'(a) = 0$ & Horizontal tangent (possible max/min) \\
\hline
$f'(a) > 0$ & Function increasing at $x = a$ \\
\hline
$f'(a) < 0$ & Function decreasing at $x = a$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Physical Interpretations:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|p{9cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{If $s(t)$ is...} & \textbf{Then...} \\
\hline
Position & $v(t) = s'(t)$ is velocity \\
\hline
Velocity & $a(t) = v'(t) = s''(t)$ is acceleration \\
\hline
Any quantity $Q(t)$ & $Q'(t)$ is rate of change of $Q$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Formulas to Remember:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Tangent line: $y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)$
    \item Normal line slope: $m_n = -\dfrac{1}{f'(a)}$
    \item Velocity: $v = \dfrac{ds}{dt}$
    \item Acceleration: $a = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = \dfrac{d^2s}{dt^2}$
    \item Average rate: $\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}$
    \item Instantaneous rate: $f'(a)$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Confusing tangent line with normal line
    \item Forgetting negative reciprocal for perpendicular slopes
    \item Mixing up position, velocity, and acceleration
    \item Confusing average and instantaneous rates of change
\end{itemize}

\newpage

If $f(x) = x^n$ where $n$ is any real number, then:
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(x^n) = nx^{n-1}}
\]

\textbf{Special Cases:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $f(x) = c$ (constant), then $f'(x) = 0$
    \item If $f(x) = x$, then $f'(x) = 1$
    \item If $f(x) = x^2$, then $f'(x) = 2x$
    \item If $f(x) = x^3$, then $f'(x) = 3x^2$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof:}}

We prove this for $n$ a positive integer using the binomial theorem.

Let $f(x) = x^n$. By definition:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{(x+h)^n - x^n}{h}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 1:} Expand $(x+h)^n$ using the binomial theorem:
\[
(x+h)^n = x^n + nx^{n-1}h + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^{n-2}h^2 + \cdots + h^n
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Substitute into the limit:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{x^n + nx^{n-1}h + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^{n-2}h^2 + \cdots + h^n - x^n}{h} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{nx^{n-1}h + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^{n-2}h^2 + \cdots + h^n}{h}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Factor out $h$ from numerator:
\begin{align*}
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{h\left(nx^{n-1} + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^{n-2}h + \cdots + h^{n-1}\right)}{h} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \left(nx^{n-1} + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^{n-2}h + \cdots + h^{n-1}\right)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Evaluate the limit as $h \to 0$:

All terms with $h$ vanish:
\[
f'(x) = nx^{n-1}
\]

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} For positive integer $n$, $\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n) = nx^{n-1}$. \qed

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note:}} The proof can be extended to negative integers, rational numbers, and all real numbers using more advanced techniques.

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $c$ is a constant and $f$ is differentiable, then:
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}[cf(x)] = c \cdot f'(x)}
\]

\textbf{In words:} The derivative of a constant times a function is the constant times the derivative of the function.

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions, then:

\textbf{Sum Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}[f(x) + g(x)] = f'(x) + g'(x)}
\]

\textbf{Difference Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}[f(x) - g(x)] = f'(x) - g'(x)}
\]

\textbf{In words:} The derivative of a sum (or difference) is the sum (or difference) of the derivatives.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of each function:

(a) $f(x) = x^5$

(b) $g(x) = x^{10}$

(c) $h(x) = x^{-3}$

(d) $k(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = x^5$

Using the power rule with $n = 5$:
\[
f'(x) = 5x^{5-1} = 5x^4
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 5x^4}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $g(x) = x^{10}$

Using the power rule with $n = 10$:
\[
g'(x) = 10x^{10-1} = 10x^9
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{g'(x) = 10x^9}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $h(x) = x^{-3}$

Using the power rule with $n = -3$:
\[
h'(x) = -3x^{-3-1} = -3x^{-4} = -\frac{3}{x^4}
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{h'(x) = -\frac{3}{x^4}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(d)} $k(x) = \sqrt[3]{x} = x^{1/3}$

Using the power rule with $n = \frac{1}{3}$:
\[
k'(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^{1/3 - 1} = \frac{1}{3}x^{-2/3} = \frac{1}{3x^{2/3}} = \frac{1}{3\sqrt[3]{x^2}}
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{k'(x) = \frac{1}{3\sqrt[3]{x^2}}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Pattern:}} Always reduce the exponent by 1 and multiply by the original exponent!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of:

(a) $f(x) = 3x^4 + 2x^2 - 5x + 7$

(b) $g(x) = 4x^3 - 6x^2 + 2$

(c) $h(x) = \dfrac{2}{x^3} + 5\sqrt{x}$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = 3x^4 + 2x^2 - 5x + 7$

Apply sum/difference and constant multiple rules:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}(3x^4) + \frac{d}{dx}(2x^2) - \frac{d}{dx}(5x) + \frac{d}{dx}(7) \\
&= 3 \cdot 4x^3 + 2 \cdot 2x - 5 \cdot 1 + 0 \\
&= 12x^3 + 4x - 5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 12x^3 + 4x - 5}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $g(x) = 4x^3 - 6x^2 + 2$

\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}(4x^3) - \frac{d}{dx}(6x^2) + \frac{d}{dx}(2) \\
&= 4 \cdot 3x^2 - 6 \cdot 2x + 0 \\
&= 12x^2 - 12x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{g'(x) = 12x^2 - 12x}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $h(x) = \dfrac{2}{x^3} + 5\sqrt{x}$

First rewrite using exponents:
\[
h(x) = 2x^{-3} + 5x^{1/2}
\]

Now differentiate:
\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= 2 \cdot (-3)x^{-4} + 5 \cdot \frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\
&= -6x^{-4} + \frac{5}{2}x^{-1/2} \\
&= -\frac{6}{x^4} + \frac{5}{2\sqrt{x}}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{h'(x) = -\frac{6}{x^4} + \frac{5}{2\sqrt{x}}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Key Technique:}} Always rewrite roots and fractions as powers before differentiating!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ for:

(a) $y = x^5 + x$

(b) $y = \dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$ (we'll need quotient rule later, but try expanding first if possible)

(c) $y = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 1$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $y = x^5 + x$

\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= 5x^4 + 1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = 5x^4 + 1}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $y = \dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$

For this, we'll need the quotient rule (covered in Section 2.4). Let's note it for now.

\textbf{Answer:} (Requires quotient rule - see next section)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $y = 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 12x + 1$

\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2(3x^2) - 3(2x) - 12(1) + 0 \\
&= 6x^2 - 6x - 12
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = 6x^2 - 6x - 12}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Note:}} This is related to past paper 2024 Q4(a) where we need to find inflection points!

\vspace{0.5cm}

The derivatives of the basic trigonometric functions are:

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\sin x) = \cos x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\cos x) = -\sin x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\tan x) = \sec^2 x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\cot x) = -\csc^2 x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\sec x) = \sec x \tan x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\csc x) = -\csc x \cot x}
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important:}} $x$ must be in radians for these formulas to be valid!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative:

(a) $f(x) = 3\sin x + 2\cos x$

(b) $g(x) = x^2 + \tan x$

(c) $h(x) = 5\sin x - 4\cos x + x^3$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = 3\sin x + 2\cos x$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 3 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(\sin x) + 2 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(\cos x) \\
&= 3\cos x + 2(-\sin x) \\
&= 3\cos x - 2\sin x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 3\cos x - 2\sin x}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $g(x) = x^2 + \tan x$

\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}(x^2) + \frac{d}{dx}(\tan x) \\
&= 2x + \sec^2 x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{g'(x) = 2x + \sec^2 x}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $h(x) = 5\sin x - 4\cos x + x^3$

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= 5\cos x - 4(-\sin x) + 3x^2 \\
&= 5\cos x + 4\sin x + 3x^2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{h'(x) = 5\cos x + 4\sin x + 3x^2}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textbf{Exponential Functions:}

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(e^x) = e^x}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(a^x) = a^x \ln a \quad (a > 0, a \neq 1)}
\]

\textbf{Logarithmic Functions:}

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\ln x) = \frac{1}{x} \quad (x > 0)}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\log_a x) = \frac{1}{x \ln a} \quad (x > 0, a > 0, a \neq 1)}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Special Property:}}

The function $e^x$ is its own derivative! This makes $e$ the natural base for exponential functions.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative:

(a) $f(x) = 3e^x + x^2$

(b) $g(x) = \ln x + 2x$

(c) $h(x) = e^x + \ln x - 5x + 4$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $f(x) = 3e^x + x^2$

\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 3 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(e^x) + \frac{d}{dx}(x^2) \\
&= 3e^x + 2x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = 3e^x + 2x}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $g(x) = \ln x + 2x$

\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}(\ln x) + 2 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(x) \\
&= \frac{1}{x} + 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{g'(x) = \frac{1}{x} + 2}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $h(x) = e^x + \ln x - 5x + 4$

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= e^x + \frac{1}{x} - 5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{h'(x) = e^x + \frac{1}{x} - 5}

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions, then:
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}[f(x) \cdot g(x)] = f(x) \cdot g'(x) + g(x) \cdot f'(x)}
\]

\textbf{Alternative notation:}
\[
(fg)' = fg' + gf'
\]

or
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(uv) = u\frac{dv}{dx} + v\frac{du}{dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

The derivative of a product is: (first function)(derivative of second) + (second function)(derivative of first).

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Error:}}

$(fg)' \neq f'g'$ ! The derivative of a product is NOT the product of derivatives!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Proof:}}

Let $h(x) = f(x) \cdot g(x)$. We use the definition of the derivative.

\textbf{Step 1:} Write the definition
\[
h'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{h(x+h) - h(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Substitute $h(x) = f(x)g(x)$
\[
h'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h)g(x+h) - f(x)g(x)}{h}
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Add and subtract $f(x+h)g(x)$ (clever trick!)
\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h)g(x+h) - f(x+h)g(x) + f(x+h)g(x) - f(x)g(x)}{h}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Factor and separate
\begin{align*}
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h)[g(x+h) - g(x)] + g(x)[f(x+h) - f(x)]}{h} \\
&= \lim_{h \to 0} \left[f(x+h) \cdot \frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h} + g(x) \cdot \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\right]
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 5:} Evaluate the limits

Since $f$ is differentiable, it's continuous, so $\lim_{h \to 0} f(x+h) = f(x)$.

\begin{align*}
h'(x) &= \lim_{h \to 0} f(x+h) \cdot \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h} + g(x) \cdot \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \\
&= f(x) \cdot g'(x) + g(x) \cdot f'(x)
\end{align*}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Conclusion:}} $(fg)' = fg' + gf'$ \qed

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of $f(x) = x^2 \sin x$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

This is a product of two functions: $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x) = \sin x$.

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify the parts
\begin{align*}
u &= x^2 \quad \implies \quad u' = 2x \\
v &= \sin x \quad \implies \quad v' = \cos x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply the product rule

$(uv)' = uv' + vu'$

\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}(x^2 \sin x) &= x^2 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(\sin x) + \sin x \cdot \frac{d}{dx}(x^2) \\
&= x^2 \cdot \cos x + \sin x \cdot 2x \\
&= x^2 \cos x + 2x \sin x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = x^2 \cos x + 2x \sin x}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Check:}} We can factor: $f'(x) = x(x\cos x + 2\sin x)$ if needed.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ if $y = e^x \sin x$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify the factors
\begin{align*}
u &= e^x \quad \implies \quad u' = e^x \\
v &= \sin x \quad \implies \quad v' = \cos x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply product rule
\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= e^x \cdot \cos x + \sin x \cdot e^x \\
&= e^x \cos x + e^x \sin x \\
&= e^x(\cos x + \sin x)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = e^x(\cos x + \sin x)}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Note:}} We factored out $e^x$ for a cleaner form.

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions and $g(x) \neq 0$, then:
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right] = \frac{g(x) \cdot f'(x) - f(x) \cdot g'(x)}{[g(x)]^2}}
\]

\textbf{Alternative notation:}
\[
\left(\frac{u}{v}\right)' = \frac{vu' - uv'}{v^2}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Memory Aid - "Lo-dee-hi minus hi-dee-lo over lo-lo":}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Lo (bottom) times dee-hi (derivative of top)
    \item Minus
    \item Hi (top) times dee-lo (derivative of bottom)
    \item All over
    \item Lo-lo (bottom squared)
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Order Matters!}}

The order in the numerator is crucial: $vu' - uv'$, NOT $uv' - vu'$!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of $f(x) = \dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$.

(From 2025 past paper, Q1 part v)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify numerator and denominator
\begin{align*}
u &= x + 1 \quad \implies \quad u' = 1 \\
v &= x - 2 \quad \implies \quad v' = 1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply quotient rule

$\left(\dfrac{u}{v}\right)' = \dfrac{vu' - uv'}{v^2}$

\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{x+1}{x-2}\right) &= \frac{(x-2)(1) - (x+1)(1)}{(x-2)^2} \\
&= \frac{x - 2 - x - 1}{(x-2)^2} \\
&= \frac{-3}{(x-2)^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{f'(x) = \frac{-3}{(x-2)^2}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Observation:}} The derivative is always negative (since $(x-2)^2 > 0$), meaning this function is always decreasing where it's defined!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of $g(x) = \dfrac{\sin x}{1 + \cos x}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Identify parts
\begin{align*}
u &= \sin x \quad \implies \quad u' = \cos x \\
v &= 1 + \cos x \quad \implies \quad v' = -\sin x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Apply quotient rule
\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{(1 + \cos x)(\cos x) - (\sin x)(-\sin x)}{(1 + \cos x)^2} \\
&= \frac{\cos x + \cos^2 x + \sin^2 x}{(1 + \cos x)^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Simplify using $\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1$
\begin{align*}
g'(x) &= \frac{\cos x + 1}{(1 + \cos x)^2} \\
&= \frac{1 + \cos x}{(1 + \cos x)^2} \\
&= \frac{1}{1 + \cos x}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{g'(x) = \frac{1}{1 + \cos x}}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Tip:}} Always look for simplification opportunities using trig identities!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} If $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable, find $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right)$.

(From 2024 past paper, Q1 part ix - statement of quotient rule)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

This is asking us to state the quotient rule.

\textbf{Answer:} 
\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right) = \frac{g(x) \cdot f'(x) - f(x) \cdot g'(x)}{[g(x)]^2}}
\]

provided $g(x) \neq 0$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In words:}} 

The derivative of a quotient equals:
\[
\frac{\text{(bottom)(derivative of top)} - \text{(top)(derivative of bottom)}}{\text{(bottom)}^2}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ if $y = \dfrac{x^2 + 1}{x} \cdot \sin x$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

This involves both a quotient and a product.

\textbf{Method 1:} Simplify first, then differentiate

Rewrite:
\[
y = \left(\frac{x^2 + 1}{x}\right) \sin x = \left(x + \frac{1}{x}\right) \sin x = x\sin x + \frac{\sin x}{x}
\]

Now differentiate each term using product rule:

For $x\sin x$:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(x\sin x) = x\cos x + \sin x
\]

For $\dfrac{\sin x}{x}$ (quotient rule):
\[
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{\sin x}{x}\right) = \frac{x\cos x - \sin x}{x^2}
\]

Therefore:
\[
\frac{dy}{dx} = x\cos x + \sin x + \frac{x\cos x - \sin x}{x^2}
\]

\textbf{Method 2:} Use product rule with $u = \dfrac{x^2+1}{x}$ and $v = \sin x$

First find $u'$ using quotient rule:
\[
u' = \frac{x(2x) - (x^2+1)(1)}{x^2} = \frac{2x^2 - x^2 - 1}{x^2} = \frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2}
\]

Then apply product rule:
\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= u \cdot v' + v \cdot u' \\
&= \frac{x^2+1}{x} \cdot \cos x + \sin x \cdot \frac{x^2-1}{x^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer (simplified):} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = x\cos x + \sin x + \frac{x\cos x - \sin x}{x^2}}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Note:}} Both methods give the same answer (verify by simplifying!). Choose the method that seems easier for the given problem.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 2.4 Summary}}

\textbf{Product Rule:}
\[
\boxed{(fg)' = fg' + gf'}
\]

\textbf{Quotient Rule:}
\[
\boxed{\left(\frac{f}{g}\right)' = \frac{gf' - fg'}{g^2}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{When to Use Which Rule:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{3cm}|p{5cm}|p{5cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Situation} & \textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Example} \\
\hline
Two functions multiplied & Product Rule & $x^2 \sin x$ \\
\hline
Function divided by function & Quotient Rule & $\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$ \\
\hline
Can simplify first & Do it! & $\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x$ \\
\hline
Second derivative & Differentiate twice & $f''(x)$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Strategy Tips:}}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Always check if you can simplify BEFORE applying product/quotient rule
    \item For quotient rule: write it as $\dfrac{\text{lo} \cdot \text{dee hi} - \text{hi} \cdot \text{dee lo}}{\text{lo}^2}$
    \item For product rule: $(uv)' = u \cdot v' + v \cdot u'$ (order doesn't matter for addition)
    \item Always simplify your final answer
    \item Use trig identities when applicable
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $(fg)' = f'g'$ \textcolor{red}{WRONG!} Use product rule!
    \item $\left(\dfrac{f}{g}\right)' = \dfrac{f'}{g'}$ \textcolor{red}{WRONG!} Use quotient rule!
    \item Getting the sign wrong in quotient rule (it's $gf' - fg'$, not $fg' - gf'$)
    \item Forgetting to square the denominator in quotient rule
    \item Not simplifying algebraically before differentiating
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Quick Check:}}

Can you state these rules from memory?
\begin{itemize}
    \item Product rule: $(uv)' = ?$
    \item Quotient rule: $\left(\dfrac{u}{v}\right)' = ?$
\end{itemize}

If not, practice until you can!

