The \textbf{linearization} of a function $f$ at $x = a$ is the linear function:
\[
\boxed{L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)}
\]

This is the equation of the tangent line to $f$ at $x = a$.

For $x$ near $a$, we have the \textbf{linear approximation}:
\[
\boxed{f(x) \approx L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The tangent line is the best linear approximation to the curve
    \item Near the point of tangency, the line and curve are very close
    \item The farther from $a$, the worse the approximation
    \item This is also called the \textbf{tangent line approximation}
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Why This Works:}}

For small $\Delta x = x - a$:
\[
f(a + \Delta x) \approx f(a) + f'(a)\Delta x
\]

The change in $f$ is approximately the derivative times the change in $x$.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the linearization of $f(x) = x + \dfrac{1}{x}$ at the point $x = 1$.

(From 2023 past paper, Q1 part xi)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

The linearization is $L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ where $a = 1$.

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $f(1)$
\[
f(1) = 1 + \frac{1}{1} = 1 + 1 = 2
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f'(x)$
\begin{align*}
f(x) &= x + \frac{1}{x} = x + x^{-1} \\
f'(x) &= 1 - x^{-2} = 1 - \frac{1}{x^2}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Find $f'(1)$
\[
f'(1) = 1 - \frac{1}{1^2} = 1 - 1 = 0
\]

\textbf{Step 4:} Write the linearization
\begin{align*}
L(x) &= f(1) + f'(1)(x - 1) \\
&= 2 + 0(x - 1) \\
&= 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{L(x) = 2}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Interpretation:}} Since $f'(1) = 0$, the tangent line is horizontal at $x = 1$. This means the function has a local extremum at this point!

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Approximation:}} For $x$ near 1, $f(x) \approx 2$. For example, $f(1.1) \approx 2$ (actual value: $1.1 + \frac{1}{1.1} \approx 2.009$).

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use linear approximation to estimate $\sqrt{4.1}$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

We want to approximate $f(x) = \sqrt{x}$ near $x = 4$ (since $\sqrt{4} = 2$ is known).

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up the problem

Let $f(x) = \sqrt{x}$, $a = 4$, and we want to approximate $f(4.1)$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f(4)$
\[
f(4) = \sqrt{4} = 2
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Find $f'(x)$ and $f'(4)$
\begin{align*}
f(x) &= x^{1/2} \\
f'(x) &= \frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}} \\
f'(4) &= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{4}} = \frac{1}{2(2)} = \frac{1}{4}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Apply linear approximation

\begin{align*}
f(4.1) &\approx f(4) + f'(4)(4.1 - 4) \\
&= 2 + \frac{1}{4}(0.1) \\
&= 2 + 0.025 \\
&= 2.025
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\sqrt{4.1} \approx 2.025}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Check:}} Using a calculator, $\sqrt{4.1} = 2.024845...$, so our approximation is very close! Error $< 0.001$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Strategy:}} Choose $a$ to be a point where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f(a)$ is easy to calculate
    \item $a$ is close to the desired $x$ value
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} The radius of a sphere is measured to be 10 cm with a possible error of $\pm 0.1$ cm. Use differentials to estimate the maximum error in the calculated volume.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

The volume of a sphere is $V = \dfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3$.

\textbf{Step 1:} Find the differential $dV$

\begin{align*}
\frac{dV}{dr} &= \frac{4}{3}\pi \cdot 3r^2 = 4\pi r^2
\end{align*}

Therefore:
\[
dV = 4\pi r^2 \, dr
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Substitute known values

Given: $r = 10$ cm, $dr = \pm 0.1$ cm

\begin{align*}
dV &= 4\pi (10)^2 (\pm 0.1) \\
&= 4\pi (100)(\pm 0.1) \\
&= \pm 40\pi \\
&\approx \pm 125.66 \text{ cm}^3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} The maximum error in volume is approximately \boxed{\pm 126 \text{ cm}^3}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Interpretation:}} A small error in radius ($0.1$ cm) leads to a much larger error in volume ($\approx 126$ cm$^3$) because volume depends on $r^3$.

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Relative Error:}}

The actual volume is $V = \dfrac{4}{3}\pi(10)^3 \approx 4188.79$ cm$^3$.

Relative error: $\dfrac{126}{4188.79} \approx 0.03 = 3\%$

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 2.7 Summary}}

\textbf{Linearization at } $x = a$:
\[
\boxed{L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)}
\]

\textbf{Linear Approximation:}
\[
\boxed{f(x) \approx f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) \quad \text{for } x \text{ near } a}
\]

\textbf{Differentials:}
\[
\boxed{dy = f'(x) \, dx}
\]

\textbf{Change Approximation:}
\[
\boxed{\Delta y \approx dy = f'(x) \, dx}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Applications:}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{4cm}|p{9cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Application} & \textbf{Method} \\
\hline
Approximate function values & Use $f(x) \approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ with $a$ near $x$ \\
\hline
Estimate errors & Use $\Delta y \approx dy = f'(x) \, dx$ \\
\hline
Sensitivity analysis & $dy$ shows how sensitive $y$ is to changes in $x$ \\
\hline
Tangent line equation & $y = L(x)$ is the tangent line \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important Notes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Linear approximation is only accurate for $x$ close to $a$
    \item The farther from $a$, the larger the error
    \item $dy$ approximates $\Delta y$ but they are not equal
    \item Always specify units when working with physical quantities
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Quick Formula Check:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Linearization includes: $f(a)$, $f'(a)$, and $(x-a)$ 
    \item Differential $dy$ includes: $f'(x)$ and $dx$ 
\end{itemize}

