\textbf{Logarithmic differentiation} is a technique used to differentiate functions of the form:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Products with many factors
    \item Quotients with complicated numerators/denominators
    \item Functions with variables in both base and exponent
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Procedure:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Take the natural logarithm of both sides: $\ln y = \ln[f(x)]$
    \item Use logarithm properties to simplify
    \item Differentiate both sides implicitly with respect to $x$
    \item Solve for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$
    \item Substitute back $y = f(x)$ if needed
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Key Logarithm Properties:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\ln(ab) = \ln a + \ln b$
    \item $\ln\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right) = \ln a - \ln b$
    \item $\ln(a^n) = n\ln a$
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Remember:}}

When differentiating $\ln y$ with respect to $x$:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(\ln y) = \frac{1}{y} \cdot \frac{dy}{dx}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative of $y = \dfrac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{(x+1)^3}$.

(From 2022 past paper, Q5 part b)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Take natural logarithm of both sides

\[
\ln y = \ln\left(\frac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{(x+1)^3}\right)
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Use logarithm properties to expand

\begin{align*}
\ln y &= \ln(x\sqrt{x^2+1}) - \ln(x+1)^3 \\
&= \ln x + \ln\sqrt{x^2+1} - 3\ln(x+1) \\
&= \ln x + \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1) - 3\ln(x+1)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$

Left side:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(\ln y) = \frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx}
\]

Right side:
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\ln x + \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1) - 3\ln(x+1)\right] &= \frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{2x}{x^2+1} - 3 \cdot \frac{1}{x+1} \\
&= \frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{3}{x+1}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 4:} Set them equal

\[
\frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{3}{x+1}
\]

\textbf{Step 5:} Solve for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$

\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= y\left(\frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{3}{x+1}\right) \\
&= \frac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{(x+1)^3}\left(\frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{3}{x+1}\right)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{(x+1)^3}\left(\frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{3}{x+1}\right)}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Advantage:}} Logarithmic differentiation converts multiplication/division into addition/subtraction, making complex derivatives manageable!

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ if $y = x^x$ (where $x > 0$).

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

This function has a variable base AND exponent, so standard rules don't apply. Use logarithmic differentiation.

\textbf{Step 1:} Take natural logarithm

\[
\ln y = \ln(x^x) = x\ln x
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Differentiate both sides

Left side:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(\ln y) = \frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx}
\]

Right side (use product rule):
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}(x\ln x) &= x \cdot \frac{1}{x} + \ln x \cdot 1 \\
&= 1 + \ln x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Solve for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 + \ln x \\
\frac{dy}{dx} &= y(1 + \ln x) \\
&= x^x(1 + \ln x)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = x^x(1 + \ln x)}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} At $x = 1$: $\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 1^1(1 + \ln 1) = 1(1 + 0) = 1$.

This makes sense because $y = x^x$ passes through $(1, 1)$ with slope 1.

\vspace{0.5cm}

If $f$ is a one-to-one differentiable function with inverse $f^{-1}$, and if $f'(f^{-1}(a)) \neq 0$, then:

\[
\boxed{(f^{-1})'(a) = \frac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(a))}}
\]

\textbf{Alternative form:} If $y = f^{-1}(x)$, then:

\[
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{dx/dy}}
\]

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{In Words:}}

The derivative of the inverse function at $a$ is the reciprocal of the derivative of the original function at the corresponding point.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}

The graphs of $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are reflections across the line $y = x$. Their tangent lines are also reflections, so their slopes are reciprocals.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\arcsin x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\arccos x) = -\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}(\arctan x) = \frac{1}{1+x^2}}
\]

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Domain Restrictions:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\arcsin x$ and $\arccos x$: defined for $|x| < 1$
    \item $\arctan x$: defined for all real $x$
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Let $f(x) = x^3 + 1$. Find $(f^{-1})'(2)$.

(Related to 2021 past paper, Q1 part xi)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

We use the formula: $(f^{-1})'(a) = \dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(a))}$

\textbf{Step 1:} Find $f^{-1}(2)$

We need to solve $f(x) = 2$:
\begin{align*}
x^3 + 1 &= 2 \\
x^3 &= 1 \\
x &= 1
\end{align*}

So $f^{-1}(2) = 1$.

\textbf{Step 2:} Find $f'(x)$

\[
f'(x) = 3x^2
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Evaluate $f'(f^{-1}(2)) = f'(1)$

\[
f'(1) = 3(1)^2 = 3
\]

\textbf{Step 4:} Apply the formula

\[
(f^{-1})'(2) = \frac{1}{f'(1)} = \frac{1}{3}
\]

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{(f^{-1})'(2) = \frac{1}{3}}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} We can find $f^{-1}(x)$ explicitly:
\begin{align*}
y &= x^3 + 1 \\
x^3 &= y - 1 \\
x &= \sqrt[3]{y - 1}
\end{align*}

So $f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[3]{x - 1}$.

Then:
\[
(f^{-1})'(x) = \frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{-2/3} = \frac{1}{3\sqrt[3]{(x-1)^2}}
\]

At $x = 2$:
\[
(f^{-1})'(2) = \frac{1}{3\sqrt[3]{(2-1)^2}} = \frac{1}{3\sqrt[3]{1}} = \frac{1}{3}
\]

Confirmed! \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find the derivative of:

(a) $y = \arcsin(2x)$

(b) $y = \arctan(x^2)$

(c) $y = x\arcsin x$

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{(a)} $y = \arcsin(2x)$

Use chain rule:
\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-(2x)^2}} \cdot 2 \\
&= \frac{2}{\sqrt{1-4x^2}}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{1-4x^2}}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $y = \arctan(x^2)$

Use chain rule:
\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{1}{1+(x^2)^2} \cdot 2x \\
&= \frac{2x}{1+x^4}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{2x}{1+x^4}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $y = x\arcsin x$

Use product rule:
\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= x \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \arcsin x \cdot 1 \\
&= \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \arcsin x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \arcsin x}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ if $y = (x^2 + 1)^x$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

The base and exponent both involve $x$, so use logarithmic differentiation.

\textbf{Step 1:} Take natural logarithm

\[
\ln y = \ln[(x^2+1)^x] = x\ln(x^2+1)
\]

\textbf{Step 2:} Differentiate both sides

Left side:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(\ln y) = \frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx}
\]

Right side (use product rule):
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}[x\ln(x^2+1)] &= x \cdot \frac{2x}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1) \cdot 1 \\
&= \frac{2x^2}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1)
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Solve for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{2x^2}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1) \\
\frac{dy}{dx} &= y\left[\frac{2x^2}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1)\right] \\
&= (x^2+1)^x\left[\frac{2x^2}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1)\right]
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{\frac{dy}{dx} = (x^2+1)^x\left[\frac{2x^2}{x^2+1} + \ln(x^2+1)\right]}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 2.8 Summary}}

\textbf{Logarithmic Differentiation Steps:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Take $\ln$ of both sides
    \item Use log properties to expand
    \item Differentiate implicitly
    \item Solve for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$
    \item Substitute back $y = f(x)$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{When to Use Logarithmic Differentiation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Products with many factors
    \item Complicated quotients
    \item Variable in both base and exponent: $f(x)^{g(x)}$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Inverse Function Derivative:}
\[
\boxed{(f^{-1})'(a) = \frac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(a))}}
\]

\textbf{Inverse Trig Derivatives:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Function} & \textbf{Derivative} \\
\hline
$\arcsin x$ & $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ \\
\hline
$\arccos x$ & $-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ \\
\hline
$\arctan x$ & $\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Common Mistakes:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forgetting to multiply by $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ when differentiating $\ln y$
    \item Not using chain rule with inverse trig functions
    \item Confusing $(f^{-1})'$ with $\dfrac{1}{f'}$ (they're not the same!)
    \item Forgetting to substitute back $y = f(x)$ in logarithmic differentiation
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Key Formulas to Memorize:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\dfrac{d}{dx}(\ln y) = \dfrac{1}{y}\dfrac{dy}{dx}$
    \item $\ln(a^n) = n\ln a$
    \item $\ln(ab) = \ln a + \ln b$
    \item $\ln\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right) = \ln a - \ln b$
\end{itemize}

